        "id": "stage-9-topic-4-section-13",
        "title": "AWS IAM Permissions — Production Registry Operations",
        "content": "## AWS IAM Permissions — Production Registry Operations\n\n### Core technical explanation\n\nECR authorization combines AWS IAM identity policies and repository resource policies. A principal that authenticates to ECR needs ecr:GetAuthorizationToken. Publishing commonly requires repository actions such as ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability, ecr:InitiateLayerUpload, ecr:UploadLayerPart, ecr:CompleteLayerUpload, and ecr:PutImage. Pulling commonly requires ecr:BatchGetImage and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer. The authorization-token action is not scoped to an individual repository in the same way as repository operations, so policy design must account for its resource requirements. Scope repository actions to the intended repository ARN. A CI role should receive push permissions only for repositories it owns. Runtime identities should receive pull permissions only where required by the selected deployment authentication design. Diagnose AccessDenied errors by checking the caller identity, requested action, resource ARN, identity policies, repository policies, permission boundaries, session policies, and organization service control policies. Never solve a narrow ECR permission failure by granting permanent AdministratorAccess.\n\n### Section-specific learning objective\n\nExplain the architecture components, their responsibilities, request flow, manifest and layer behavior, and the impact on image availability and performance.\n\n### Practical implementation and commands\n\nThe following commands demonstrate the relevant ECR workflow. Replace ACCOUNT_ID, ROLE_NAME, repository names, image tags, and Region with values from the target AWS environment. Review the target resource and permissions before running configuration-changing commands.\n\naws sts get-caller-identity\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/ROLE_NAME --action-names ecr:PutImage --resource-arns arn:aws:ecr:ap-south-1:ACCOUNT_ID:repository/payments-api\n\n### CI/CD integration\n\nIn a production pipeline, use a dedicated IAM role and short-lived credentials. Keep repository configuration and IAM policies in version control. Build the image from a known source revision, publish it using a unique release identifier, and capture the resulting digest. Stop the pipeline if authentication, publication, image verification, or required security checks fail. Promote the same verified artifact between environments rather than rebuilding a different image for every environment.\n\n### Verification and troubleshooting\n\nVerify the result using AWS CLI responses, repository metadata, image tags, image digest, and relevant CloudTrail events. If an operation fails, capture the exact error, caller identity, Region, repository name, resource ARN, and requested action. Check IAM identity policies, repository policies, KMS permissions, network connectivity, and tag mutability as applicable. Correct the specific cause and repeat the verification using the same role that the pipeline or runtime uses.\n\n### Production security and reliability\n\nApply least-privilege permissions, immutable release tags, digest-based deployments, vulnerability scanning, encryption, lifecycle retention, and audit logging. Protect known-good images needed for rollback. Document repository ownership, publishing permissions, image promotion approvals, and the operational response to compromised or vulnerable artifacts. Review these controls whenever the application's release process or AWS account architecture changes."
      },
      {
